findings.body:
`SelectedCalendarRepository.ts` renames the exported class to `PrismaSelectedCalendarRepository`, but nothing else in the PR was updated to match. Two callers that are outside this diff still import the old name from the same module path:

- `apps/web/app/api/cron/calendar-subscriptions/route.ts` line 11 and its use at line 41
- `apps/web/app/api/webhooks/calendar-subscription/[provider]/route.ts` line 14 and its use at line 61

The repository test file has the same problem at line 3 (`import { SelectedCalendarRepository }`) and line 53.

At runtime, `new SelectedCalendarRepository(prisma)` becomes `new undefined(...)` and throws `TypeError: ... is not a constructor`, so the cron job and webhook fail on every invocation. The TypeScript build also fails. The test file's `import` resolves to `undefined`, so every test in it fails, including the new generic-calendar test. A rename this broad should not appear in a feature PR. If it is needed, all importers have to change in the same commit.

::diff{file="packages/features/selectedCalendar/repositories/SelectedCalendarRepository.ts" lines="1-6"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The routes that break are not in the diff, so GitHub will not flag them. The class name is the only thing that changed in these lines, so reverting it is the smallest fix.

::code{file="apps/web/app/api/cron/calendar-subscriptions/route.ts" lines="40-42"}

`GENERIC_CALENDAR_SUFFIXES` is keyed by provider, which suggests each provider's suffixes should apply only to that provider's rows. `getGenericCalendarSuffixes()` ignores the keys and flattens every list into one array. The result is then applied to the whole `integration: { in: integrations }` batch.

For now this is mostly harmless because Office 365 has an empty list and Google's suffixes do not appear in Office 365 IDs. It still breaks the design. Adding a provider with suffixes that happen to match another provider's externalId format would silently filter the wrong rows, and the per-provider structure implies scoping that the code does not do.

::diff{file="packages/features/calendar-subscription/adapters/AdaptersFactory.ts" lines="67-70"}

::callout{type="info"}
If the filter really should be global, flatten the map once and say so in the name. If it should be provider-scoped, make the repository accept a per-integration mapping instead of a flat list.

`genericCalendarSuffixes` is optional in both the interface and the implementation. When a caller omits it, the query runs with no generic-calendar filter at all, and nothing signals the omission. The current service always passes it, but the cron route, the webhook route, and any future caller can each leave it out and get the old behavior silently.

The filter exists to prevent a specific data-quality problem, so the safer default is for the repository to apply the constant when no list is supplied, or for the parameter to be required. Making it required would turn a forgotten argument into a compile error.

::diff{file="packages/features/selectedCalendar/repositories/SelectedCalendarRepository.ts" lines="18-45"}

::callout{type="warning"}
An empty array also disables the filter here. The `?.length` check treats `[]` the same as `undefined`, which is fine for Office 365 today, but it means that a misconfigured empty map silently removes all filtering.

`AdaptersFactory.test.ts` is unchanged in this PR, so `getGenericCalendarSuffixes()` has no direct test. Nothing checks that it returns Google's four suffixes, that the Office 365 entry contributes nothing, or that the flattening keeps all of them.

The `CalendarSubscriptionService` tests also hard-code a copy of the four suffixes in four separate places. They will keep passing if someone edits `GENERIC_CALENDAR_SUFFIXES`, so they do not protect the real list.

::diff{file="packages/features/calendar-subscription/adapters/AdaptersFactory.ts" lines="67-70"}

The ternary checks `genericCalendarSuffixes?.length` for truthiness, which already excludes `undefined`. The `?.` in `genericCalendarSuffixes?.map` is therefore unnecessary and makes the reader wonder whether `undefined` can reach that branch. A plain `genericCalendarSuffixes.map` is clearer.

::suggestion{file="packages/features/selectedCalendar/repositories/SelectedCalendarRepository.ts" lines="41-45"}
        AND: genericCalendarSuffixes?.length
          ? genericCalendarSuffixes.map((suffix) => ({
              NOT: { externalId: { endsWith: suffix } },
            }))
          : undefined,

The new `where` clause builds `AND: [{ NOT: { externalId: { endsWith: suffix } } }]`, one entry per suffix. The `externalId` column is declared as a non-null `String` on `SelectedCalendar`, so SQL three-valued logic does not turn `NOT LIKE` into NULL for any row. No row is dropped by accident.

The `AND: undefined` shape used when no suffixes are passed is also correct. Prisma ignores undefined keys, so the existing queries keep their old behavior, and the tests assert this.

::diff{file="packages/features/selectedCalendar/repositories/SelectedCalendarRepository.ts" lines="41-45"}
